PRE-TABLE CHECK -- sgideprimop (#500), the draw_primitive() default arm
Written 2026-08-31 BEFORE any table runs; this file's mtime is the order proof.
Baseline: HEAD 3ded1e8, src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c unmodified (catch-all still exit(1)).
Fix under test: decline + latched announce, KEEPING the message text and appending "(once)".

Nine seats answered pass 1 and the flagship adjudicated; this block is written from that
panel, not from memory.  #498 skipped this block and its own pass-2 panel then measured two
escapes that #490's block had prescribed BY NAME.

--------------------------------------------------------------------- 1. SIBLING PARITY
The nearest sibling is in the SAME struct and one function away: the #498 width decline in
the sgi_de access function.  Shape:

    if (!(d->warned & SGIRE_W_DE_LEN)) { d->warned |= SGIRE_W_DE_LEN;
        fatal("[ sgi_de: unimplemented len=%i -- declined.  (once) ]\n", (int) len); }
    goto de_declined;

ASYMMETRY FOUND, and it is a row we owe rather than a defect: the sibling JUMPS to a common
tail (goto de_declined) because its enclosing function HAS one.  draw_primitive() has NO
tail -- verified: one-tab brace closes the switch, column-0 brace closes the function, next
line is DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de).  So break here is equivalent to falling off the end, and the
per-case-return idiom that broke four readers on a sibling round is NOT a hazard here.
Three seats refused to grant that premise from the packet; it was settled by reading.

SECOND ASYMMETRY, and this one is the round's: the sibling's guard field and its announce
are BOTH about width.  Here the guard field is a latch bit while the guarded body reads
register state -- see check 3.

--------------------------------------------------------------------- 2. CLAIM -> ROW
Added-comment claims this round will make, each owing a row or an explicit no-row sentence:

  "POINT (0x00) and TRIANGLE (0x02) are documented primitives that reach this arm"
        -> ROW: two sessions, one per encoding, each expecting the decline and survival.
  "op reads the RESET value, so a START access before any PRIMITIVE write draws POINT"
        -> ROW: a session that writes ONLY the START-aliased address and never
           CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE.  This is the reachability headline and it must be pinned.
  "the decline is announced once per device instance"
        -> ROW: two DISTINCT defaulted primitives in one session, expecting exactly ONE
           message.  This is also mutant M1's killer -- see check 4.
  "a START-aliased READ also draws, because if (startFlag) is not conditioned on MEM_WRITE"
        -> ROW: the read direction, separately.  Named by a seat; no existing row covers it.

NO-ROW SENTENCE OWED, recorded rather than left blank: the [31:26] stray-bit finding -- a
legal LINE with a high bit set also dies -- is NOT pinned by this round.  Whether a guest
ever sets those bits is not established, so a row would assert a reachability nobody has
shown.  A deliberate no-row with its reason, which must not render the same as an unrun one.

------------------------------------------------- 3. GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD
  guard reads: d->warned  (one bit)
  body  reads: op, x1, y1, x2, y2, fg, bg, pattern, drawmode, stipple_mode -- all out of
               d->de_reg[], none of them the guard field

The fields DISAGREE completely: the guard is a latch, the body is register state.  The
question the rule asks -- are they written at the same sites? -- answers NO, and that is
exactly what makes M3 below possible.

  d->warned write sites: 7 today (grep -c 'd->warned |=' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c), rising
  to 9 after #499 (bits 7, 8) and this round (bit 9).

  *** BIT ALLOCATION IS DECONFLICTED IN ADVANCE: #499 takes 1<<7 and 1<<8; THIS ROUND TAKES
  1<<9.  Both rounds were designed for 1<<7 and the collision was caught by listing the enum
  before writing either fix.  A duplicate enumerator is legal C and silent, and both devices
  cast the same struct, so d->warned is shared. ***

--------------------------------------------------------------------------- 4. CHANNEL
Per mutant: the row that kills it, and the observable that differs under it.

  M1 dropbang    if (!(d->warned & BIT))  ->  if ((d->warned & BIT))
                 The latch never sets from the zeroed initial state, so the announce never
                 fires -- while the guard still READS correct and the host still survives.
                 CHANNEL: the message count.  KILLED BY the two-distinct-primitives row
                 (wants 1, gets 0).

  M2 bitalias    1 << 9  ->  1 << 6, aliasing SGIRE_W_DE_LEN.
                 CHANNEL: the message, suppressed by prior UNRELATED traffic.  KILLED BY a
                 session that fires the existing latch sites FIRST, then triggers POINT.
                 A fresh-device primitive-only test CANNOT see this -- that is the whole
                 point, and it is why the row needs the pre-arm.

  M3 assign      d->warned |= BIT  ->  d->warned = BIT.  One character.  Clears every OTHER
                 latch.  CHANNEL: a PREVIOUSLY-fired sibling message firing AGAIN.  KILLED
                 BY an A -> primitive -> A session requiring A's message exactly once.
                 This is check 3's field disagreement made concrete.

  M4 exitzero    exit(1) reinstated as exit(0) at the default arm.
                 *** THIS IS THE ONE THAT MAKES THE OBVIOUS ORACLE VACUOUS, AND IT IS
                 KNOWABLE HERE RATHER THAN AFTER A CLEAN TABLE. ***  The natural re-pin of
                 tail_acc_x is exit1 -> exit0.  Under M4 the session announces once and then
                 DIES: how is "exit0", the re-pinned row reads GREEN, tail_acc still counts
                 1, and no other session reaches draw_primitive().  TOTAL ESCAPE.
                 EXIT STATUS CANNOT VARY between "declined and quit" and "died with status
                 0", so that oracle is VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION at this site.
                 KILLED BY re-pinning onto a POSITIVE POST-DRAW OBSERVABLE: extend the
                 session with a load through the device and assert the VALUE (printed()
                 already reads "died" when the host is gone).  An exit-status row may remain
                 as a SECOND channel; the value row is the load-bearing one.

ROWS THAT WILL RED BY SUCCEEDING, with their tiers.  The oracle-correction re-pin lands
FIRST and the committed build is measured RED under the new expectations BEFORE any edit:

  tail_acc_x    CERTAIN   wants exit1.  See M4 for what it must NOT be re-pinned to.
  tail_acc      NO        counts the DRAW substring and wants 1.  DRAW is a PREFIX of the
                          fatal and "(once)" appends AFTER it, and the session is a fresh
                          process issuing exactly one draw -- so first = only = 1.  Green,
                          no re-pin, PROVIDED the text is kept.
  hi_draw,      NO but    both want count(DRAW) == 0, and their declined-width paths bypass
  hi_rd_draw    FRAGILE   draw_primitive() anyway.  A REWORD would leave them counting a
                          dead string -- green for the wrong reason, unfailable, invisible
                          to every gate.  This is the strongest argument for keeping the text.
  sgi_de rows   CERTAIN   gate_arm.sh checks a literal 29.  When tail_acc_x reds, that count
  green (29)              falls to 28 and the denominator check reds too.  Bump it in the
                          SAME edit, or the round meets two reds and misattributes one.

CHECKS: 4 run, 0 skipped.  Findings: one mutant (M4) whose obvious oracle is vacuous by
construction, caught at writing time; one deliberate no-row recorded with its reason; one
latch-bit collision deconflicted in advance; one third gate observer that two file-reading
seats missed.
PRETABLE_DONE

