AMENDMENT, 2026-08-31 ~05:30 -- STILL BEFORE TABLE 1.  No table has run for this
round, so this block continues to precede the measurement it governs; the mtime
order the rule depends on is intact and this is not a back-write.

Added after the ninth seat answered the stage's held question and found a gap in
section 2 that I had missed.

--------------------------------------------- 5. THE ACCEPT SIDE -- OWED, AND MISSING
*** SECTION 2 ENUMERATES FIVE ROWS AND NOT ONE OF THEM IS AN ACCEPT ROW.  That is
not coverage debt; it is the de-escalation clause's own obligation, which says the
detector shipping WITH a fix that removes a host-fatal guard must pin the ACCEPT
SIDE of each predicate the guard used to gate -- "part of the FIX'S COST, not
future strengthening". ***

The predicate that gates death here is exactly

    (op & 0xff000000) is one of { DE_PRIM_LINE, DE_PRIM_RECTANGLE }

so the accept side is: a LEGAL primitive must still draw.  Nothing in this round
tested that, and the reason it went unnoticed is measured rather than guessed:

    grep -rn 'PRIMITIVE|0x2060|0x01000000|0x03000000' \
         regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py \
         regress/sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py            ->  ZERO hits

NOT ONE PROBE IN THE TREE PROGRAMS CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE OR ISSUES A LEGAL LINE OR
RECTANGLE.  Every row this round prescribes issues POINT, TRIANGLE, or the reset
value -- i.e. only the DECLINE side.

  M5 primdrop    delete the `case DE_PRIM_RECTANGLE:' LABEL, so RECTANGLE falls
                 through into the de-fatalised default: arm.  ONE LINE.
                 Plausible rather than contrived: the four documented primitives
                 INTERLEAVE (implemented 0x01/0x03, unimplemented 0x00/0x02), so
                 a label edit is an ordinary future slip.
                 Not covered by M1-M4: the latch guard is intact, the bit value is
                 intact, `|=' is intact, and there is NO DEATH at all -- it is
                 strictly quieter than M4's exit(0).
                 CHANNEL: before this round, deleting that label meant exit(1) on
                 the guest's first rectangle -- unmissable.  AFTER the fix it means
                 one (once) line and a console that never renders text, since
                 dev_sgi_re.c records at :760-761 that OpenBSD draws characters
                 through this mechanism.  THAT IS THE ACCIDENTAL TRIPWIRE THIS FIX
                 DESTROYS, and it is exactly what the de-escalation clause exists
                 to make the round pay for.
                 KILLED BY a session programming a legal RECTANGLE with
                 DE_DRAWMODE_ROP (0x00000200) set and rop = an unimplemented value
                 (OPENGL_LOGIC_OP_OR = 7), asserting BOTH:
                     count(DRAW) == 0                              and
                     count("[ sgi_de: RECT: rop[") == 1
                 The second is load-bearing: a bare count(DRAW)==0 is survival-only
                 and stays green if the whole switch is skipped.  That string is
                 emitted at :798 from inside the RECTANGLE arm AND NOWHERE ELSE
                 (verified: two hits in the file, :732 LINE and :798 RECT), so it
                 is an arm-unique anchor needing no framebuffer read-back.
                 A matching LINE row at :732 costs one more session.
                 CAVEAT, and it decides the session shape: rop_used is
                 FUNCTION-STATIC, so the anchor fires once per PROCESS.  Fresh-
                 process rows are fine; one session issuing both LINE and RECTANGLE
                 must use DIFFERENT rop values.

--------------------------------- 6. LATCH SCOPE -- COPY THE SHAPE, NOT THE SCOPE
The in-file precedent for a latched announce at a switch default: is real and is
what licenses this fix -- the ROP default arms at :721-738 and :787-804 announce
without exiting, and the XFER guard at :637-644 has its exit(1) commented out.

BUT THE PRECEDENT IS BAD IN ONE RESPECT AND THE FIX MUST DEPART FROM IT:

    grep -rn 'static int first = 1;|static char .*_used\[' src/devices/*.c
      -> 4 lines, ALL in dev_sgi_re.c, ALL inside draw_primitive()

`static char rop_used[256]` and `static int first` are FUNCTION-STATIC, i.e.
process-global and shared by every device instance -- the n_trunc_msgs
anti-pattern this project has already recorded as the tree's one bad model.  They
are the only such sites in the whole devices tree.  d->warned + "(once)" is the
house form.  COPYING THE NEIGHBOUR'S SCOPE WOULD BE THE MISTAKE; COPYING ITS
SHAPE IS RIGHT.

A SECOND TENSION, recorded rather than resolved: the in-file precedent latches PER
VALUE (rop_used[256] gives one announce per rop), while this fix latches ONE BIT
for 254 op classes -- so POINT-then-TRIANGLE prints once.  That is deliberate (it
is M1's killer row, and it matches #498's recorded accepted cost), but an
(op >> 24)-indexed table would be equally cheap and the nearest neighbour in the
same function chose exactly that.  THE ROUND MUST SAY WHY IT DID NOT, or a future
reader reads the difference as an oversight rather than a choice.

CHECKS: 6 run, 0 skipped.  AMENDED FINDINGS: one FLOOR-CLAUSE obligation missing
from the original block (the accept side), with its mutant and its arm-unique
kill channel; one latch-scope hazard in the very precedent that licenses the fix.
PRETABLE_AMENDED_DONE
